}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by {{field-1}} both sides of  the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting {{field-3}} into {{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ To get $\\text{L2},$ we are $\\text{differentiating}$ with respect to $t$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}^{′}(t) & =(Φu(t))^{′} \\\\ {x}_{p}^{′}(t) & =Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ To get $\\text{L7},$ we are $\\text{integrating}$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L7},$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Substituting\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φu(t) \\\\  & =Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "66c525c7384960b0b65a7996258bdbf3e74f102a04bd513d66b6071c9db68eb2"} {"question" "q-336716" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336716" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "adding $Φ$ to"] ["text" "differentiating"] ["text" "integrating"] ["text" "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336716" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "differentiating"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336716" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336716" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "adding $Φ$ to"] ["text" "differentiating"] ["text" "integrating"] ["text" "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336716" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "integrating"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336716" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336716" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\text{L2}"] ["math" "\\text{L5}"] ["math" "\\text{L6}"] ["math" "\\text{L7}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336716" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\text{L7}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336716" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336716" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\text{L1}"] ["math" "\\text{L3}"] ["math" "\\text{L4}"] ["math" "\\text{L5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336716" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\text{L1}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-330877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}} matrix.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-330877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L4}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ i.e., by substituting any column $c(t)$ of $Φ$ into the homogeneous system, we get\n\n$$\n{c}^{′}(t)=Ac(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}^{′}(t)$ is the corresponding column of derivatives in $Φ.$ As a result, this means that ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that $Φ\\text{ is a fundamental}$ matrix.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are linearly independent. So, $Φ$ is invertible. Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{multiplying by }{Φ}^{-1}$ both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ ({Φ}^{-1}Φ)\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ To get $\\text{L7},$ we are $\\text{integrating}$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-330877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "66c525c7384960b0b65a7996258bdbf3e74f102a04bd513d66b6071c9db68eb2"} {"question" "q-330877" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-330877" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ is an invertible"] ["text" "$A$ is the identity"] ["text" "$Φ$ is a fundamental"] ["text" "$Φ$ is a square"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-330877" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$Φ$ is a fundamental"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-330877" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-330877" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "adding $Φ$ to"] ["text" "differentiating"] ["text" "integrating"] ["text" "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-330877" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-330877" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-330877" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "adding $Φ$ to"] ["text" "differentiating"] ["text" "integrating"] ["text" "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-330877" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "integrating"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337230" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $2{e}^{-t}$ |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337230" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{4t}-0\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -8{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337230" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "66c525c7384960b0b65a7996258bdbf3e74f102a04bd513d66b6071c9db68eb2"} {"question" "q-337230" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337230" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-8e^{-4t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337230" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337230" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-330899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & -6{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | ${e}^{-t}$ |  |  | $9$ |  |  |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-330899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & -6{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-6{e}^{2t})-5{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}[\\begin{aligned}-6{e}^{2t} & -5{e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-6{e}^{2t} & -5{e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-6{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-6{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}12{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 12{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & -6{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5{e}^{t} \\\\ 9{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-330899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "66c525c7384960b0b65a7996258bdbf3e74f102a04bd513d66b6071c9db68eb2"} {"question" "q-330899" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-330899" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12e^{4t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-330899" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-330899" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & -4{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}$ |  |  | $-5$ |  |  |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & -4{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-4{e}^{-3t})-5{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{2t}}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-3t} & -5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ -{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-3t} & -5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ -{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-2t} & -5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-2t} & -5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{20}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & -4{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{20}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{t} \\\\ -5{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "66c525c7384960b0b65a7996258bdbf3e74f102a04bd513d66b6071c9db68eb2"} {"question" "q-337281" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337281" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{22}{5}e^{-t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337281" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337281" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $5{e}^{-t}$ |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $-4$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{3t}-({e}^{3t})\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -3{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "66c525c7384960b0b65a7996258bdbf3e74f102a04bd513d66b6071c9db68eb2"} {"question" "q-336699" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336699" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3e^{-3t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336699" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-336699" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}$ |  |  | $\\frac{17}{6}$ |  |  |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-3{e}^{4t})-3{e}^{4t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}}[\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{4t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{4t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{17}{6}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{17}{6}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "66c525c7384960b0b65a7996258bdbf3e74f102a04bd513d66b6071c9db68eb2"} {"question" "q-337280" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337280" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5e^{2t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337280" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337280" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-329817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}12t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-12t-6$ |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-329817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}12t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-2{e}^{t}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 8t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}-4t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 8t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int -4t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 8t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=-4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -4t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-4t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (-4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}+\\int 2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=8t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=8\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -4t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =8t\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot 8\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8t{e}^{-t}+\\int 8\\,{e}^{-t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8t{e}^{-t}-8{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =-8{e}^{-t}(1+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\ -8{e}^{-t}(1+t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1-2t)-16(1+t) \\\\ 2(1-2t)-8(1+t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18t-15 \\\\ -12t-6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-329817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "66c525c7384960b0b65a7996258bdbf3e74f102a04bd513d66b6071c9db68eb2"} {"question" "q-329817" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-329817" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-18t-15"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$ |  | $30t+20$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & 5{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-5{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -5{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{-2t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -5{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}\\cdot (\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{5}{6}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-\\frac{1}{2}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-\\frac{1}{2}t)\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & 5{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}5(3t-1)+45(t+1) \\\\ 5(3t-1)+9(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}30t+20 \\\\ 12t+2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "66c525c7384960b0b65a7996258bdbf3e74f102a04bd513d66b6071c9db68eb2"} {"question" "q-337307" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337307" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12t+2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $6t-6$ |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 3t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}-2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 3t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int -2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=-2t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-2\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (-2\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-t{e}^{2t}+\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=3t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(3t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =3t{e}^{t}-3{e}^{t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\ 3{e}^{t}(t-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}(1-2t)+6(t-1) \\\\ 6(t-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4t-5 \\\\ 6t-6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" 