l that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot 3{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{-4t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(4t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{3}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}(-\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-t{e}^{t}+\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t{e}^{t}+{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(1-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ {e}^{t}(1-t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}4(3t-1)+9(1-t) \\\\ 4(3t-1)+27(1-t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3t+5 \\\\ 23-15t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "66c525c7384960b0b65a7996258bdbf3e74f102a04bd513d66b6071c9db68eb2"} {"question" "q-337286" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337286" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3t+5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $39t+23$ |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot 3{e}^{t}-{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{-2t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{3}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-t)\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int (-{e}^{-t})\\cdot (-\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}-\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ {e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}4(3t-1)+9(t+1) \\\\ 4(3t-1)+27(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}21t+5 \\\\ 39t+23\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "66c525c7384960b0b65a7996258bdbf3e74f102a04bd513d66b6071c9db68eb2"} {"question" "q-337306" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "q-337306" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "21t+5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/state.json" "file_sha256" "def80725fba71073c5b2744717a688ccc0498c6afbbf4b41a6e1ece31b50aebe"} {"question" "e-21546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) =\nA\n\\mathbf{x}(t) +\n\\mathbf{f}(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\mathbf{u}(t),$ where $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for $\\mathbf x_p(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\:$ $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\mathbf{u}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\:$ $\\mathbf{x}'_p(t) = \\Phi \\mathbf{u}'(t) + \\Phi' \\mathbf{u}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\:$ $\\Phi \\mathbf{u}'(t) + \\Phi' \\mathbf{u}(t) = A \\Phi \\mathbf{u}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\:$ $\\Phi' = A \\Phi$\n\n$\\textrm{L5}{:}\\:$ $\\Phi \\mathbf{u}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L6}{:}\\:$ $\\mathbf{u}'(t) = \\Phi^{-1}(t) \\mathbf{f}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L7}{:}\\:$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t$\n\n$\\textrm{L8}{:}\\:$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\int \\Phi^{-1}\\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t$\n\nFill in the blanks with the correct reasons to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from the fact that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/81e146c6f6297f8f30340d63ddaec02efe3d6442005f5852a70d5be22ceed764.png) matrix.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L5}$ by  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/09febb48bb00d22caebf088103181b5db6377befccad18f1370718351cc91e9d.png) both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\textrm{L7}$ into ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/9be3bba9e4f49ae67d635fe7b6260607d3e776f03baeaf8d5a10d650e0b0b987.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/example-21546.json" "file_sha256" "602b250a75081227deacf1e7adb2b29ba82cb499e5ad3b0a22d0fe67353de6d7"} {"question" "e-21546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L4}.$ Since $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) =A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ i.e., by substituting any column $\\mathbf{c}(t)$ of $\\Phi$ into the homogeneous system, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}'(t) =A\\mathbf{c}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{c}'(t)$ is the corresponding column of derivatives in $\\Phi.$ As a result, this means that $\\Phi' = A \\Phi.$ Therefore, $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from the fact that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/f3cf7b9339acd3d9b370c67a182509bff354728494afda00e2e991f3a09996af.png) matrix.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\textrm{L5}$ and $\\textrm{L6}.$ Since $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are linearly independent. So, $\\Phi$ is invertible. Therefore, $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}$ by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/8ba4d14f89d4c90c90f84e0618b0cf8dedadbf2c3989cc9533e44590100357d1.png) both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ ({Φ}^{-1}Φ)\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L1},$ $\\textrm{L7},$ and $\\textrm{L8}.$ Substituting\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom $\\textrm{L7}$ into $\\textrm{L1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φu(t) \\\\  & =Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\textrm{L7}$ into  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/4d1fa76e9619981c1b36c457c5247dad023bdbb7f7810fdc9e5b9105028c4e22.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/example-21546.json" "file_sha256" "602b250a75081227deacf1e7adb2b29ba82cb499e5ad3b0a22d0fe67353de6d7"} {"question" "e-21547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/2b22742201ba6932de40a628419faef0405bc8ae0ad26814bc7ca515455e3e5c.png)\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute $\\Phi^{-1} \\, \\mathbf{f}(t)$ and the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ of the system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/example-21547.json" "file_sha256" "6ee550256cebf15c0f774cb5948ccaedb5984b58ea831f2215a590666dc55b97"} {"question" "e-21547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\, \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/0c7accec33fc7c9d6b2a228c8ec4bcc454f687be7f96ab615b3b818f82fb8cc8.png)\n\nSince $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-2{e}^{-4t})-2{e}^{-4t}\\cdot {e}^{3t}}[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-4t} & -2{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-4t} & -2{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $\\mathbf{u}(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2{e}^{-4t} \\\\ {e}^{3t} & -2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{t} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{10}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{10}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/example-21547.json" "file_sha256" "6ee550256cebf15c0f774cb5948ccaedb5984b58ea831f2215a590666dc55b97"} {"question" "e-21548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/bc92d310e939c8688ab770a51107d1218d5b8878a877d7b3a64af770d002a9ca.png)\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/860251c7cc4c1259c4660b41aae1f3e0b0177e13c9372dd941422040f0f7e5ee.png) |  | $\\boxed{\\rule{0pt}{1.3em}\\phantom{xxxxxxxx}}$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/example-21548.json" "file_sha256" "f8449140ddc92799dca694830a19a2042b4aa3e006aaab25cf8fd4e978ac016c"} {"question" "e-21548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{x}_c(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/e8db6a28a4671312259595de0609cefb8db0d5fc441e122885335e1165e0cd22.png)\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\, \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/assets/742a0ea79220f0dc0055918b611393d69518bc5c73ef9b41b6fd068c8c75ad4c.png)\n\nSince $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{-4t}-0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $\\mathbf{u}(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 0\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=2t$ and $\\text{d}v=e^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=2\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(2\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =t{e}^{2t}-\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(2t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, the integrand is zero, so the integral is zero.\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(2t-1) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071383/example-21548.json" "file_sha256" "f8449140ddc92799dca694