unctions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for $\\mathbf x_p(t)$ is given below.\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\:$ $\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\mathbf{u}(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\:$ $\\mathbf{x}'_p(t) = \\Phi \\mathbf{u}'(t) + \\Phi' \\mathbf{u}(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\:$ $\\Phi \\mathbf{u}'(t) + \\Phi' \\mathbf{u}(t) = A \\Phi \\mathbf{u}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\:$ $\\Phi' = A \\Phi$\n> $\\textrm{L5}{:}\\:$ $\\Phi \\mathbf{u}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L6}{:}\\:$ $\\mathbf{u}'(t) = \\Phi^{-1}(t) \\mathbf{f}(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L7}{:}\\:$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t$\n> $\\textrm{L8}{:}\\:$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\int \\Phi^{-1}\\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t$\n\nFill in the blanks with the correct reasons to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from the fact that $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$ matrix.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L5}$ by $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$ both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\textrm{L7}$ into $AAAAAAAAA$"] [17592186166456 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L4}$. Since $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) =A\\mathbf{x}(t)$, i.e., by substituting any column $\\mathbf{c}(t)$ of $\\Phi$ into the homogeneous system, we get\n$\\mathbf{c}'(t) =A\\mathbf{c}(t)$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{c}'(t)$ is the corresponding column of derivatives in $\\Phi$. As a result, this means that $\\Phi' = A \\Phi$. Therefore, $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from the fact that $Φ$ matrix.\n- Next, we consider lines $\\textrm{L5}$ and $\\textrm{L6}$. Since $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are linearly independent. So, $\\Phi$ is invertible. Therefore, $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}$ by $Φ^{-1}$ both sides of the equation:\n$Φ^{-1} \\cdot Φu^{′}(t)|= Φ^{-1} \\cdot f(t); (Φ^{-1}Φ) \\cdot u^{′}(t)|= Φ^{-1} \\cdot f(t); u^{′}(t)|= Φ^{-1} \\cdot f(t)$\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L1}$, $\\textrm{L7}$, and $\\textrm{L8}$. Substituting\n$\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t$\nfrom $\\textrm{L7}$ into $\\textrm{L1}$, we get\n$x_{p}(t)|= Φu(t); = Φ∫Φ^{-1}f(t)dt$.\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\textrm{L7}$ into $L1$"] [17592186166457 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute $\\Phi^{-1} \\, \\mathbf{f}(t)$ and the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ of the system."] [17592186166457 :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\, \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2e^{-4t} \\\\ e^{3t} & -2e^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ^{-1} &= (1)/(0 \\cdot (-2e^{-4t}) - 2e^{-4t} \\cdot e^{3t})\\begin{bmatrix}-2e^{-4t} & -2e^{-4t} \\\\ -e^{3t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{e^{t}}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2e^{-4t} & -2e^{-4t} \\\\ -e^{3t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{-3t} & e^{-3t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}e^{4t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nΦ^{-1}f(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{-3t} & e^{-3t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}e^{4t} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ 2e^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3e^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $\\mathbf{u}(t)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(t) &= ∫Φ^{-1}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫\\begin{bmatrix}3e^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}dt \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}∫3e^{-2t}dt \\\\ ∫\\frac{1}{2}e^{5t}dt\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2}e^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{p}(t) &= Φu(t) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2e^{-4t} \\\\ e^{3t} & -2e^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2}e^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}e^{t} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{10}e^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= e^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{10}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186166458 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ |  | $\\boxed{\\rule{0pt}{1.3em}\\phantom{xxxxxxxx}}$ |  | . |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $0$ |  |  |  |"] [17592186166458 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{x}_c(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\, \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{-2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ^{-1} &= \\frac{1}{e^{-2t} \\cdot e^{-4t} - 0}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{-4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= e^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{-4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}. \\\\\nΦ^{-1}f(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2te^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $\\mathbf{u}(t)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(t) &= ∫Φ^{-1}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫\\begin{bmatrix}2te^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}dt \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}∫2te^{2t}dt \\\\ ∫0dt\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=2t$ and $\\text{d}v=e^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t$. Then, $\\text{d}u=2\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2t}$. So,\n$∫2te^{2t}dt|= (2t) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}e^{2t} - ∫\\frac{1}{2}e^{2t}(2dt); = te^{2t} - ∫e^{2t}dt; = te^{2t} - \\frac{1}{2}e^{2t}; = \\frac{1}{2}e^{2t}(2t - 1)$.\n- For the second component, the integrand is zero, so the integral is zero.\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{p}(t) &= Φu(t) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{-2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}e^{2t}(2t - 1) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t - 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186164089 17592186164090 17592186164091 17592186164092 17592186164094 17592186164095 17592186164096 17592186164097 17592186164099 17592186164100 17592186164101 17592186164102 17592186164710 17592186164711 17592186164712 17592186164713 17592186164715 17592186164716 17592186164717 17592186164718 17592186164720 17592186164721 17592186164722 17592186164723 17592186164725 17592186164726 17592186164727 1759